    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Phoenician was spoken in the cities of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and carried by Tyro-Sidonian trade and colonies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of the maritime world in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</li><li>Its alphabet, an abjad that wrote only consonants, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and is generally believed to be at least a partial ancestor of almost all modern alphabets.</li><li>Almost nothing written on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> or parchment survived the damp coasts, so the language is known from about 10,000 inscriptions; a bilingual found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> let Jean-Jacques Barthélemy decipher it in 1758.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word gorilla comes from Punic: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanno-the-navigator">Hanno the Navigator</a>’s interpreters gave the name to a tribe of hostile ‘hairy people’ in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, it passed into Greek as gorillai, and in 1847 Thomas S. Savage applied it to the western gorilla.</p></aside>
    </section>

